Subject: Read/Write CDs as possible student storage for Athena?

      Hello. You have recieved this message at the request of cec that I
let you know what I'm doing this summer, becuase you might be
potentially interested in the results, if I am successful.

      This summer I will be spending a good deal of time UROPing at the
new Art and Media Technology center, with the Architecture Machine
Group. I will be designing a unix-compatiable file-server/file-system
for use with Write-Once Compact Disks.

      Some of the unique features of the CDs are that they store an
incredible amount of information (on the order of 1GB) and that once a
sector on the CD is written, it cannot be altered back to its original
state. The file system that I am writing exploits these capiabli1ites to
allow the user to access all previous revisions of his document, access
files that have already been deleted, and simple perform simple backup
procedures. In the final system, users will get files from their personal
CD, modify them, and then the files back to the CD. The directory of the
CD will be kept on the CD, in addition to the data. Each user will have
his own CD, which will cost less than $50.

      Using a system such as this, it should be possible for a user to
save his 100 page thesis to his CD well over a thousand times. If the CD
ever fills up, it will be trivial to save the most current version of
the document to a virgin CD and start over again. My estimate is that a
single CD will last the average MIT student well over his four year stay
at the Institute.

      I submit that if there was a small write-once CD player next to
every Project Athena workstation, this would make the ultimate system
for user file storage. It features unlimited user storage, total
security, and frees the Athena opperations from doing backups on user
files. In each cluster there would be two workstations with two CD
units, to allow users to do their own backups.

      I hope to also write a package which would allow IBM/XTs with
ethernet boards to act as CD servers.

      I would enjoy discussing the preliminary planning with anybody who
is interested. I am very eager to hear feedback.

                              Simson L. Garfinkel

